// `pure` and `mutable self` are a contradiction, and the reason is not symmetry with the existing
// rule about `mutable` PARAMETERS — it is the same rule, reaching the receiver.
//
// `pure` says the answer depends on the arguments and nothing else, which is the same thing as
// changing nothing. `mutable self` says this call changes the receiver. A signature asserting both
// is asserting in two directions at once.
//
// And it matters more than it looks, which is why the message says so: **a contract clause may call
// a `pure` method**, so allowing this would let a precondition rewrite the very value it is checking.
class Counter { n: Int }

pure function (mutable self: Counter) bump() -> Int {
    self.n = self.n + 1;
    return self.n;
}

region r {
    let mutable c: Counter = Counter { n: 1 };
    print(c.bump());
}
